{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790966787,"nonce":"nXC7zYUfsp0z2X9nYk2C/f9u3Ng=","title":"Blinc DSL `computed { … } : T` bindings rendered 0. The #[ignore] message blaming 'upstream","message":"`dsl_opacity_computed_binding_reflects_signal_changes` (f64) and `dsl_bg_computed_binding_reflects_signal_changes` (i32) are `#[ignore]`d with *\"blocked on upstream zyntax lambda {f64,i32}-return propagation\"*. **That attribution is wrong** — verified against BOTH the pinned rev (`9763886`) and a local zyntax checkout; both still fail, and the upstream pieces are all present:\n\n- The grammar already emits the right shape: `computed_expr_f64` builds `Lambda { body: TypedExpression::Block { statements: [TypedStatement::Return(Some(e))] } }` (`grammar/blinc.zyn` ~2112). The `Return` is there.\n- zyntax's block-**expression** arm (`compiler/src/ssa.rs`, `TypedExpression::Block`) explicitly handles `Return(Some(e)) =\u003e last_val = val`, and routes other control flow through `process_statement`. Its own comment documents that arm being fixed previously.\n- zyntax's lambda compiler reads the node type: `TypedExpression::Lambda(lambda) =\u003e self.translate_closure(block_id, lambda, \u0026expr.ty)`.\n\n**A real Blinc-side AST bug DOES exist here, but it is not the cause of these failures.** `TypedLambda` has NO return-type field — zyntax derives the compiled signature from the Lambda *node's* `ty`: `Type::Function { return_type, .. }` is honoured, and **everything else falls back to `HirType::I64`** (`translate_closure`, the `_ =\u003e` arm). Blinc leaves that node `Primitive(Unit)` (confirmed by dumping the AST), so a `computed { … } : f64` compiles to an I64-returning function (value in `rax`) while the host FFI `blinc_dsl_computed_f64` transmutes to `extern \"C\" fn() -\u003e f64` and reads `xmm0` — a genuine ABI mismatch for f64/bool. A pass that annotates the lambda node with the correct `Type::Function` is saved at `scratchpad/computed-lambda-fn-type.patch` (adds `annotate_computed_lambda_types` to `passes/reactive.rs`; must run late — earlier passes rebuild the lambda nodes).\n\n**Narrowed by instrumenting `blinc_dsl_computed_f64` (print `func()` at the FFI boundary).** Findings, in order:\n\n1. **The read path is NOT the bug.** Pure-Rust check (no DSL/JIT): `computed::\u003cf64,_\u003e(|_| 0.25)` then `Computed::\u003cf64\u003e::from_id(id).try_get()` → `Some(0.25)`; i32 likewise. So the reactive layer registers, types, and round-trips correctly.\n2. **The FFI IS invoked** with a valid non-null closure pointer. The lambda is compiled and called.\n3. **Without the type annotation, `func()` returns denormal garbage** (observed `2.1457e-314`) — an integer bit pattern read as f64, i.e. the rax/xmm0 mismatch. **With** the annotation it returns a clean `0.0`. So the ABI fix is real and does exactly what it should: it corrects the register, and is required for f64/bool computed to ever work.\n4. **The value itself is still lost.** `computed { 0.25 } : f64` — a pure literal, no signal read — also returns 0. So the failure is not about signals, not about the read path, and not about the register: **the lambda's body value never reaches its return**.\n5. Signal-ordering was checked and exonerated: `set_signal_f64` before vs after `compile_source` both leave the signal at `Some(0.25)`.\n6. Both lambda body shapes were tried — the grammar's `LambdaBody::Expression(Block{[Return(e)]})` and zyntax's documented implicit-return form `LambdaBody::Block{[Expression(e)]}` (normalised in the pass). **Both yield 0.**\n\nSo a genuine lambda-return-propagation failure does exist, and the `#[ignore]`'s core claim is not baseless — but its attribution was still incomplete, because a separate, definite Blinc-side AST bug (missing `Type::Function`) corrupts f64 values independently and had to be fixed before the propagation failure was even visible as a clean zero.\n\n**DECISIVE TEST — DONE. The bug is 100% Blinc-side; zyntax lambda return works.** A hand-built `TypedProgram` run straight through `ZyntaxRuntime` (no Blinc grammar, no passes) returns the right value on both shapes:\n\n```\nA  fn plain_f64() -\u003e f64 { return 0.25 }              -\u003e 0.25   OK\nB  fn make_lambda() -\u003e i64 { return \u003clambda\u003e }        -\u003e ptr\n   transmute(ptr) as extern \"C\" fn() -\u003e f64, called   -\u003e 0.25   OK\n```\n\nReference test saved at `scratchpad/raw_ast_lambda_reference_test.rs` — worth landing as a permanent regression test. **The `#[ignore = \"blocked on upstream zyntax lambda …-return propagation\"]` message on both tests is simply false; delete it when the real fix lands.**\n\nThe two ingredients that make case B work, and which Blinc's DSL path lacks:\n1. The Lambda node carries `Type::Function { return_type: f64, … }` (Blinc leaves it `Primitive(Unit)` → zyntax's `_ =\u003e` fallback picks `HirType::I64`).\n2. The body is `TypedLambdaBody::Block` whose trailing statement is a **bare `Expression`** (zyntax's documented implicit-return form). Blinc's grammar emits `LambdaBody::Expression(Block{[Return(e)]})`.\n\nApplying both to the DSL path still produced 0 in my run — but the body-shape normaliser was a silent `if let` chain, so it most likely never matched the post-pass shape rather than failing to help. **Next step: make Blinc's computed lambda match case B exactly, and assert/log that the rewrite actually fires instead of relying on a silent guard.** Do not re-test the upstream side; it is proven good.\n\nLesson: two independent sessions accepted the `#[ignore]` text as fact. Treat an `#[ignore = \"blocked on upstream…\"]` as a hypothesis, not evidence. Related: gotcha zyntax lambda return value (states the same wrong attribution), gotcha dsl control flow in view bodies.\n\n\n**RESOLVED — commit 16abb4c3.** Three stacked Blinc-side bugs: (1) grammar `computed_expr_*` built `Return(Some(e))` in tuple form; the action-language interpreter only reads the named `value` field -\u003e `Return(None)`, body discarded at parse (the `return_stmt` rule at grammar ~1210 always had the correct `Return { value: … }` form — align with it); (2) Lambda node typed `Unit` -\u003e zyntax's I64-return fallback vs host FFI reading xmm0 — fixed by `annotate_computed_lambda_types` (passes/reactive.rs), which MUST run immediately before codegen because earlier passes rebuild lambda nodes; (3) once the body ran, its global-path `Signal::try_get` re-locked the graph mutex held by `get_derived` around compute — a self-deadlock (this was the \"57-minute hang\": cargo at 0% CPU in wait4 on the hung TEST BINARY, not a slow build or cache lock; diagnose with `sample \u003cpid\u003e`). Fixed by mirroring run_effect's IN_FLIGHT_GRAPH guard into `get_derived` (blinc_core reactive.rs) — the in-flight read also registers the dependency, which is what makes the derived re-fire on set. Un-ignored tests needed unique signal names (\"alpha_cmp\"/\"bg_color_cmp\"): the process-global signal registry makes name reuse race across parallel tests. Raw-AST reference test landed at crates/blinc_dsl_core/tests/lambda_return_abi.rs.","files":null}]}